pokeplatinum-widepointers3.patch (tools/pokeplat_gen.py applies it to build/pokeplatinum, after widepointers2): a particle
system's buffer twice its DS size on a 64-bit host. Every ParticleSystem_New caller allocates the buffer itself (0x4800,
one 0x4200) and passes its size; particle_system.c's bump allocator (ParticleSystem_AllocMemory) gives the SPL library its
manager, emitters, particles and resources from it, structs with pointers that outgrow the DS size. Past the end it only
asserts, so it went on into the next heap block: in the opening (ov77) it zeroed heap 76's block headers and every later
allocation from that heap failed (frame 1062, FS_ReadFile into NULL). The rule, as heap.c's HEAP_HOST_SIZE:
PARTICLE_HEAP_HOST_SIZE(size) in particle_system.h around the size at each caller's Heap_Alloc and ParticleSystem_New.

diff --git a/include/particle_system.h b/include/particle_system.h
--- a/include/particle_system.h
+++ b/include/particle_system.h
@@ -3,6 +3,15 @@
 
 #include <nitro/fx/fx.h>
 #include <nnsys.h>
+#include <stdint.h>
+
+// A particle system's buffer holds the SPL library's manager, emitters, particles and resources, structs with pointers:
+// twice its DS size where a pointer is 8 bytes (as heap.c's HEAP_HOST_SIZE). The caller allocates it and passes its size.
+#if UINTPTR_MAX > 0xFFFFFFFFu
+#define PARTICLE_HEAP_HOST_SIZE(size) ((size) * 2)
+#else
+#define PARTICLE_HEAP_HOST_SIZE(size) (size)
+#endif
 
 #include "constants/heap.h"
 #include "constants/narc.h"
